<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  PHASE_02_RECONSTRUCTED_POC — REQUESTED_ENTITLEMENT, not GRANTED_ENTITLEMENT.

	  This file records what the PoC would ASK FOR. Nothing here is granted by
	  existing: an entitlement only exists after a profile that carries it signs the
	  build, and the free/personal-team path does not carry the JIT entitlement.

	  Consequence for the physical validation: build the IPA unsigned (as the CI
	  does), sign it however you normally sideload, and let the app REPORT what it
	  actually got. The jit suite answers with:
	      jit.map_jit_probe      PASS      -> MAP_JIT was granted
	      jit.map_jit_probe      BLOCKED   -> refused, with the real errno
	      jit.execution_allowed  PASS/BLOCKED
	  A refusal there is a finding about the signing context, not proof that the
	  port is broken.

	  Deliberately NOT wired into CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS: attaching an entitlement
	  you cannot be granted would break sideloading, and the test must measure the
	  context the build actually runs in.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
